## Request

Build the smallest complete isolated development target for the Tool Shed documentation site and
dashboard. Reuse the production service shape while preserving strict filesystem, Compose, data,
credential, port, route, and lifecycle separation.

## Work2 Boundary

- Use development root `/home/jon/docker/ts.rookaro.com-dev` and Compose project
  `tsrookarocom-dev` on `sup.local`.
- Publish plain HTTP only on `192.168.7.5:8443`; create no public DNS, Nginx Proxy Manager, or
  Rookaro router entry.
- Keep production project `tsrookarocom`, `/home/jon/docker/ts.rookaro.com`, port `8087`, its
  containers, network, volumes, environment, database, and public route unchanged.
- Use an empty development database plus deterministic synthetic seed data. Never copy production
  credentials, enrollments, sessions, personal data, or writable mounts.
- Add explicit development UI and machine-readable identity, a plain-HTTP-only development
  security profile, resource limits, scoped deploy/reset/rollback guidance, LAN checks, and a
  localhost-only persistent Workpc tunnel contract.
- Stop at the protected boundary before creating development secrets, maintainer credentials,
  TOTP material, or reporter credentials unless the operator explicitly authorizes that action.

## Verification

Run focused settings, deployment, dashboard, and documentation tests; validate the Compose model;
capture before/after production health and identity; test direct LAN access from the Linux and
Windows test beds; verify the development endpoint has no public route; and verify Workpc access
through a managed `127.0.0.1` tunnel when its host-local installation is authorized.

